<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.75%" pitch="-3.10%">
            Boaz went up to the gate and sat down there.<break time="500ms"/>
      The nearer redeemer, of whom he had spoken, came by, and Boaz said, turn aside, friend, and sit down here.<break time="500ms"/>
      And he sat down.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Then Boaz took ten men of the elders of the city and said, sit down here.<break time="500ms"/>
      And they sat down.<break time="1000ms"/>
      He explained that Naomi, who had come back from Moab, was selling the parcel of land that had belonged to Elimelech.<break time="500ms"/>
      He told the other redeemer, if you will redeem it, redeem it.<break time="500ms"/>
      But if you will not, tell me, that I may know, for there is no one besides you to redeem it, and I come after you.<break time="500ms"/>
      The man said, I will redeem it.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Then Boaz said, the day you buy the field from the hand of Naomi, you also acquire Ruth the Moabite, the widow of the dead, in order to perpetuate the name of the dead in his inheritance.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The redeemer said, I cannot redeem it for myself, lest I impair my own inheritance.<break time="500ms"/>
      Take my right of redemption yourself, for I cannot redeem it.<break time="1800ms"/>
      So the man drew off his sandal, which was the custom in Israel to confirm a transaction.<break time="500ms"/>
      And Boaz said to the elders and all the people, you are witnesses this day that I have bought all that was Elimelech's and all that was his sons'.<break time="500ms"/>
      Also Ruth the Moabite, the widow, I have bought to be my wife, to perpetuate the name of the dead in his inheritance.<break time="1000ms"/>
      All the people at the gate said, we are witnesses.<break time="500ms"/>
      May the Lord make this woman who is coming into your house like Rachel and Leah, who together built up the house of Israel.<break time="1800ms"/>
      So Boaz took Ruth, and she became his wife.<break time="500ms"/>
      And the Lord gave her conception, and she bore a son.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The women said to Naomi, blessed be the Lord, who has not left you this day without a redeemer.<break time="500ms"/>
      They named the child Obed.<break time="500ms"/>
      He was the father of Jesse, who was the father of David.<break time="1800ms"/>
      What began in emptiness and grief ended in fullness and rest.<break time="500ms"/>
      God did not speak from a burning bush in this story.<break time="500ms"/>
      He did not part any sea.<break time="500ms"/>
      He worked through a widow's loyalty, a kinsman's honour, and a handful of barley left in a field.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>
